00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:05,000
 So we've added the known secret to the end of it right here.

208
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:09,120
 Then we run it through the SHA-1 calculation and you can take--

209
00:17:09,120 --> 00:17:14,520
 look, there we go.

210
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:16,360
 And go ahead and try this if you want.

211
00:17:16,360 --> 00:17:19,280
 Maybe you have this up in a separate window, sha-1.php.

212
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:24,720
 Put Eat Less Ovaltine Santa in and you will get a different signature.

213
00:17:24,720 --> 00:17:26,920
 Because you change even a single character

214
00:17:26,920 --> 00:17:29,400
 and SHA-1 will give us a different digest.

215
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:31,960
 That's cryptographic hashes in action, right?

216
00:17:31,960 --> 00:17:36,360
 Even the tiniest change in megabytes of data

217
00:17:36,360 --> 00:17:38,480
 will change the cryptographic hash.

218
00:17:38,480 --> 00:17:42,520
 That's the beauty of SHA-1 and MD5 and SHA-256 and the others.

219
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:44,400
 There is no match.

220
00:17:44,400 --> 00:17:50,120
 So we know that this message either did not come from Annie

221
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:52,120
 or was modified in transit.

222
00:17:52,120 --> 00:17:58,800
 So we can tell the difference and we do not have to trust the medium, right?

223
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:04,560
 OK, so let's see what got coming up next here.

224
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:09,520
 OK, so here is the encryption technique.

225
00:18:09,520 --> 00:18:13,960
 And let's just stop and let you do one of these on your own.

226
00:18:13,960 --> 00:18:23,160
 And say that we've got two messages from Annie.

227
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:26,160
 And I want you to stop and I want you to calculate.

228
00:18:26,160 --> 00:18:30,600
 Santa is the secret, OK?

229
00:18:30,600 --> 00:18:32,400
 Santa is the secret.

230
00:18:32,400 --> 00:18:36,840
 And I want you to tell me if free cookies or free candy actually

231
00:18:36,840 --> 00:18:38,240
 came from Annie or not.

232
00:18:38,240 --> 00:18:41,520
 One of them is a valid message from Annie

233
00:18:41,520 --> 00:18:45,200
 and the other is not a valid message from Annie, OK?

234
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:49,200
 So I want you to take a moment, use the SHA-1 calculator,

235
00:18:49,200 --> 00:18:53,120
 and I want you to try to figure out which of these is valid

236
00:18:53,120 --> 00:18:56,320
 and which of these is not valid, OK?

237
00:18:56,320 --> 00:18:57,200
 Give you a minute.

238
00:18:57,200 --> 00:19:06,920
 OK, one last chance before we do the reveal.

239
00:19:06,920 --> 00:19:08,760
 OK, here we go.

240
00:19:08,760 --> 00:19:14,120
 So here comes a message from an insecure medium, free cookies.

241
00:19:14,120 --> 00:19:22,600
 With that as the message digest, and we got the other one, free candy,

242
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:25,200
 with that as the message digest.

243
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:33,280
 So what we do is we take off the digest and add the word Santa to each one.

244
00:19:33,280 --> 00:19:37,080
 And then we run the SHA-1 on each one.

245
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:40,640
 And then we get the two SHA-1s.

246
00:19:40,640 --> 00:19:43,080
 Here's the one and here's the two.

247
00:19:43,080 --> 00:19:46,400
 And then what we do, most importantly, is we

248
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:50,280
 compare them with the received SHA-1.

249
00:19:50,280 --> 00:19:53,280
 And when you compare them with the received SHA-1 or the received message

250
00:19:53,280 --> 00:19:55,560
 digest or the received message signature,

251
00:19:55,560 --> 00:20:00,120
 you see right away that one is good and one is bad.

252
00:20:00,120 --> 00:20:02,480
 It's as simple as that, right?

253
00:20:02,480 --> 00:20:06,000
 One of these is good and one of these is bad.

254
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:10,160
 So digital signatures are actually really surprisingly simple

255
00:20:10,160 --> 00:20:14,680
 and surprisingly easy to do without a lot of complex technology.

256
00:20:14,680 --> 00:20:16,640
 The only complex technology in here is really

257
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:21,960
 the clever mathematics that makes these cryptographic hashes work effectively.

258
00:20:21,960 --> 00:20:24,280
 The simple concatenation of a secret.

259
00:20:24,280 --> 00:20:26,480
 Now, you want your secrets to be kind of longer than this

260
00:20:26,480 --> 00:20:28,320
 and more random than that.

261
00:20:28,320 --> 00:20:30,960
 But ultimately, the notion of a digital signature

262
00:20:30,960 --> 00:20:34,760
 is actually a simple and rather elegant and beautiful notion

263
00:20:34,760 --> 00:20:38,120
 that really leverages this notion of cryptographic hashes

264
00:20:38,120 --> 00:20:40,760
 in a really cool manner.

265
00:20:40,760 --> 00:20:45,120
 So that kind of sums up our first half lecture,

266
00:20:45,120 --> 00:20:47,560
 where we really talk more about the techniques

267
00:20:47,560 --> 00:20:51,240
 of both integrity and confidentiality.

268
00:20:51,240 --> 00:20:55,680
 But we've done it all with a secret key, where we have the same key.

269
00:20:55,680 --> 00:20:59,680
 We have a moment where we're together in a secure manner

270
00:20:59,680 --> 00:21:03,480
 and we exchange the code book, whether it's Annie or the Caesar

271
00:21:03,480 --> 00:21:06,120
 or whatever, where we know what the shift is.

272
00:21:06,120 --> 00:21:11,280
 So every pair of communicating people or systems needs a key.

273
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:15,040
 Now, in the internet, with everybody buying from everybody else

274
00:21:15,040 --> 00:21:17,800
 and using credit cards, it's just not practical.

275
00:21:17,800 --> 00:21:22,280
 You just could not have a secret key for Amazon.

276
00:21:22,280 --> 00:21:24,840
 I guess it kind of works for the password.

277
00:21:24,840 --> 00:21:26,160
 We'll get to that in a second.

278
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:28,480
 The password doesn't solve everything, because then you

279
00:21:28,480 --> 00:21:31,400
 would have to actually visit Amazon to get your password set up.

280
00:21:31,400 --> 00:21:35,160
 And so the problem is we have to use an insecure medium

281
00:21:35,160 --> 00:21:39,280
 to establish the first secret, as it were.

282
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:42,000
 And so it just was never going to work.

283
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,040
 So we need a different approach for the internet.

284
00:21:44,040 --> 00:21:47,920
 And that's what we're going to talk about in the next lecture.

285
00:21:47,920 --> 00:21:48,440
 See you then.

